Review corrections — contextual core boundaries (A1-A6)

What changed:
- Rejects malformed or expired core scopes before both typed and legacy method dispatch, preventing fallback to ambient access.
- Wires the existing turn cancellation token into in-flight contextual core calls, so Stop and double Escape interrupt the nested operation.
- Enforces the contract limit of 8 concurrent core calls per HostIdentity without letting one extension consume the shared pool.
- Keeps optional host capabilities closed until a valid handshake explicitly requests them; malformed handshakes stay on the base contract.
- Preserves pending permission prompts until Rust emits permissionClosed instead of clearing every prompt on an unrelated tool result.
- Moves extension tool declarations into the existing extension module include, bringing agent_local_modules_tools.rs below the 230-line source limit without adding a new abstraction.

Root-cause choices:
- The scope guard lives in core_api_dispatch::policy because every stable, advanced and legacy dispatch route passes there.
- The quota key is HostIdentity, matching the contract and preserving the intentionally shared identity of official plugins.
- Permission lifetime remains owned by the backend PendingGuard; the frontend now consumes its explicit close event only.

